WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.610 --> 00:00:18.020
He has achieved legendary status among conmen
by pulling off some of the most outrageous,

00:00:18.020 --> 00:00:21.970
brazen and unbelievable deceits in the history
of fraud.

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:26.770
By the age of 21, he had impersonated a Pan
Am pilot, a paediatric supervising doctor

00:00:26.770 --> 00:00:31.560
and a lawyer, charming everyone he met and
bouncing checks all over the world.

00:00:31.560 --> 00:00:41.480
In this week’s Biographics, we take a look
at the incredible story of Frank William Abagnale.

00:00:41.480 --> 00:00:50.380
Early Life

00:00:50.380 --> 00:00:55.079
Frank William Abagnale was born on April 27th,
1948 in New Rochelle, New York.

00:00:55.079 --> 00:01:00.360
Named after his father, who owned a stationery
business, Frank was the third of four children.

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:03.460
His mother, Paulette, was of French-Algerian
extraction.

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:08.630
The couple met while Frank, Sr. was on active
service in Algiers during World War 2.

00:01:08.630 --> 00:01:11.750
They were soon married despite a thirteen
-year age gap.

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:15.990
Frank, Sr. proved to be a very successful
businessman with the stationery store doing

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:17.530
exceptionally well.

00:01:17.530 --> 00:01:21.640
As a result, the family lived in a large home
and, although the Abagnale’s were by no

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:23.140
means rich, Frank.

00:01:23.140 --> 00:01:26.950
Jr and his siblings had a comfortable childhood.

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:32.049
A Family Torn Apart

00:01:32.049 --> 00:01:35.310
Frank attended Ioana Catholic School in Westchester,
New York.

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:39.890
His world was turned upside down in 1960,
when his parents separated.

00:01:39.890 --> 00:01:44.420
This came as a shock to all the children,
who thought that their mother was a contented

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:47.009
housewife who had everything she ever wanted.

00:01:47.009 --> 00:01:50.170
However, Frank Sr. was very active in local
body politics, as well as being active in

00:01:50.170 --> 00:01:52.140
the management of the New York Yacht Club.

00:01:52.140 --> 00:01:54.400
In addition, he was a very keen deep-sea fisherman.

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:57.829
All of these interests meant that he was often
away from home.

00:01:57.829 --> 00:02:01.780
One day he came home from an overseas marlin
fishing adventure to find that his wife and

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:03.850
four kids had moved out.

00:02:03.850 --> 00:02:06.729
Frank Sr. was crushed by the separation.

00:02:06.729 --> 00:02:10.950
He deeply loved his wife and fought to get
her back until the day he died.

00:02:10.950 --> 00:02:13.230
But Paulette’s mind was made.

00:02:13.230 --> 00:02:17.470
As a sign of independence, she enrolled in
dental college and began building a life of

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:18.470
her own.

00:02:18.470 --> 00:02:22.380
Frank Sr. recruited Jr. to help him win back
Paulette’s affections.

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:28.400
He would give his son gifts to pass on, schooling
him just to what to say and how to say it.

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:29.400
But Paulette wouldn’t budge.

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:32.360
The couple divorced when Frank was fourteen.

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:37.750
Living with Dad

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:40.700
Unlike his siblings, Frank chose to live with
his father.

00:02:40.700 --> 00:02:45.750
This opened up a whole new world for the teenager,
who had been used to a regimented and disciplined

00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:46.920
life with his mother.

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:51.209
Tagging along with his dad, he began spending
time in the bars around New York as Frank

00:02:51.209 --> 00:02:54.840
Sr. wheeled and dealed amongst the city’s
businessmen and politicians.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:57.650
The experience quickly made the boy street-wise.

00:02:57.650 --> 00:03:01.819
It was around this time that Frank began to
skip school in order to run with a handful

00:03:01.819 --> 00:03:07.900
of local punks who were, like him, from dysfunctional
families and seeking some way to express their

00:03:07.900 --> 00:03:09.030
frustrations.

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:13.342
Frank was bigger and more mature looking than
his associates, which was handy when it to

00:03:13.342 --> 00:03:18.879
came to playing hooky – he was often mistaken
for a teacher on an outing with students.

00:03:18.879 --> 00:03:23.340
The group committed minor crimes like sneaking
into movie theaters and stealing candy.

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:27.300
Frank soon became frustrated at the childishness
and lack of sophistication of his partners

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:28.310
in crime.

00:03:28.310 --> 00:03:32.260
They didn’t exhibit any of the class that
he had realized was the key to making it as

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:33.930
a successful criminal.

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:39.050
Things came to a head when his mates stole
a car while the owner was watering his lawn.

00:03:39.050 --> 00:03:43.080
They swung by to pick Frank up only to be
stopped by the police moments later.

00:03:43.080 --> 00:03:46.209
Frank and his fellow cons were thrown into
juvenile detention.

00:03:46.209 --> 00:03:50.989
Frank Sr’s political connections managed
to get his son sprung and to have any mention

00:03:50.989 --> 00:03:53.600
of the incident erased from the record.

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:57.629
Frank’s dad told him that he was wasting
his time hanging around kids who were clearly

00:03:57.629 --> 00:04:00.650
not as mature or sophisticated as him.

00:04:00.650 --> 00:04:03.989
It was time to start acting like a man.

00:04:03.989 --> 00:04:08.540
The pep talk seemed to do the trick and Frank
began attending school regularly.

00:04:08.540 --> 00:04:12.239
He also got a part time job as a shipping
clerk at a warehouse in Bronxville.

00:04:12.239 --> 00:04:17.199
To show his support for his son’s change
of direction, Frank Sr. bought him his first

00:04:17.199 --> 00:04:20.430
car – an old Ford.

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:25.350
Wheels and Girls

00:04:25.350 --> 00:04:28.270
Having a set of wheels opened up new horizons
for young Frank.

00:04:28.270 --> 00:04:31.169
Specifically, it allowed him to meet and enjoy
the company of girls.

00:04:31.169 --> 00:04:36.469
For the next couple of years, by his own admission,
he was consumed with satisfying his teenage

00:04:36.469 --> 00:04:37.469
libido.

00:04:37.469 --> 00:04:40.569
He would cruise around town, prowling for
a young lady that he could entice with his

00:04:40.569 --> 00:04:43.979
ever-growing charm into an evening of intimate
activity.

00:04:43.979 --> 00:04:47.610
Frank soon discovered that his sexual excursions
cost money.

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:51.699
Invariably he’d have to supply his conquest
with a coke and a burger in order to keep

00:04:51.699 --> 00:04:52.699
her happy.

00:04:52.699 --> 00:04:56.999
The problem was that his meagre finances didn’t
allow for such an expense.

00:04:56.999 --> 00:05:00.639
Frank approached his dad and requested a gas
charge card.

00:05:00.639 --> 00:05:05.979
He promised that he wouldn’t abuse the privilege
and his father was impressed with his sincerity.

00:05:05.979 --> 00:05:09.119
He handed his Mobil card over to his son.

00:05:09.119 --> 00:05:12.569
A month later, Frank enacted his first scam.

00:05:12.569 --> 00:05:16.330
Pulling into a Mobil gas station he saw a
sign announcing that you can put a new set

00:05:16.330 --> 00:05:19.960
of tires on your car and charge it to your
Mobil Card.

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:24.190
It was then that he realized that you could
use the card for other things aside from gas.

00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:28.389
Thinking fast, he approached an attendant
at the station that he was familiar with.

00:05:28.389 --> 00:05:33.300
Frank asked what it would cost for a new sets
of tires – the answer was $160.

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:37.659
Frank readily acknowledged that he didn’t
need new tires but said that he was desperately

00:05:37.659 --> 00:05:38.669
short of cash.

00:05:38.669 --> 00:05:42.569
So, he suggested that he buy the set of tires
and charge them to the card.

00:05:42.569 --> 00:05:47.199
But rather than taking the tires, the attendant
would give Frank a hundred dollars for them.

00:05:47.199 --> 00:05:52.430
He could then sell off the tires for $160,
making a quick sixty-dollar profit.

00:05:52.430 --> 00:05:58.039
Ten minutes later Frank drove away from the
Mobil with a hundred dollars cash in his pocket.

00:05:58.039 --> 00:05:59.050
He was hooked.

00:05:59.050 --> 00:06:04.039
Over the next few weeks, Frank replicated
the scheme at different Mobil stations around

00:06:04.039 --> 00:06:05.039
the Bronx.

00:06:05.039 --> 00:06:08.869
At times he would simplify the deal, offering
the pump attendant to give him ten dollars

00:06:08.869 --> 00:06:12.279
of cash while signing a bill for a twenty-dollar
gas sale.

00:06:12.279 --> 00:06:17.029
It was only when the bill from Mobil arrived
that Frank realized that his father was the

00:06:17.029 --> 00:06:19.099
chump who would pay for his excesses.

00:06:19.099 --> 00:06:23.729
But Frank had a simple solution- he threw
the bills into the waste bin.

00:06:23.729 --> 00:06:27.249
Of course, that didn’t solve anything.

00:06:27.249 --> 00:06:32.120
Pretty soon, a Mobil representative visited
Frank Sr. at his store and requested an explanation

00:06:32.120 --> 00:06:41.379
for an outstanding $3,400 bill in 3 months
after 15 years of impeccable payment history.

00:06:41.379 --> 00:06:44.460
Frank Jr. was called in front of both men
to explain.

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:48.309
He said he didn’t know what they were talking
about, but neither man was convinced.

00:06:48.309 --> 00:06:51.229
Finally, Frank admitted it, but quickly offered
the ultimate excuse . . .

00:06:51.229 --> 00:06:53.849
It’s the girl’s dad – they do funny
things to me.

00:06:53.849 --> 00:06:55.259
I can’t explain it.

00:06:55.259 --> 00:06:57.960
His father nodded with a wry smile and replied
. . .

00:06:57.960 --> 00:07:00.059
Don’t worry about it, boy.

00:07:00.059 --> 00:07:02.360
Einstein couldn’t understand it either.

00:07:02.360 --> 00:07:06.619
Unfortunately for young Frank, his mother
wasn’t so understanding.

00:07:06.619 --> 00:07:11.309
She blamed his criminal activity on the influence
of his father and promptly organized for him

00:07:11.309 --> 00:07:14.399
to attend a Catholic private school for problem
children.

00:07:14.399 --> 00:07:16.189
Frank spent a year there.

00:07:16.189 --> 00:07:20.649
During those twelve months his father’s
financial fortunes took a steep reverse and

00:07:20.649 --> 00:07:22.309
he lost almost everything.

00:07:22.309 --> 00:07:27.970
He was quick to assure his son that the $3,400
that he had stolen was not a factor in his

00:07:27.970 --> 00:07:29.909
money problems.

00:07:29.909 --> 00:07:35.169
On His Own

00:07:35.169 --> 00:07:36.830
Frank left home at 16.

00:07:36.830 --> 00:07:40.770
He left without saying a word to anyone, hopping
on a train to New York City.

00:07:40.770 --> 00:07:44.719
Along with the few belongings that he’d
crammed into a suitcase, he had in his possession

00:07:44.719 --> 00:07:47.889
a check book that had $200 in the account.

00:07:47.889 --> 00:07:52.009
An hour off the train he had attached himself
to a teenage boy and convinced him to take

00:07:52.009 --> 00:07:53.059
him home with him.

00:07:53.059 --> 00:07:57.129
Frank went on to spin a story about being
an orphan and that he was determined to make

00:07:57.129 --> 00:07:58.789
it on his own in the big city.

00:07:58.789 --> 00:08:01.889
The parents offered for him a stay to as long
as he wanted it.

00:08:01.889 --> 00:08:06.490
The next day Frank got a job as a stock clerk
for $1.50 per hour.

00:08:06.490 --> 00:08:09.900
He quickly found out this was not going to
pay his way in New York.

00:08:09.900 --> 00:08:13.080
He figured that the reason for his low wage
was his age.

00:08:13.080 --> 00:08:17.179
So, he simply altered the date of birth on
his driver’s license from 1948 to 1938.

00:08:17.179 --> 00:08:20.789
Now, as a 26-year-old, he went out looking
for another job.

00:08:20.789 --> 00:08:25.409
He found one as a truck driver’ helper for
$2.75 an hour.

00:08:25.409 --> 00:08:28.189
Frank supplemented his income by cashing checks
from his account.

00:08:28.189 --> 00:08:32.260
After a couple of days, he knew that his account
was overdrawn, but that technicality didn’t

00:08:32.260 --> 00:08:33.260
phase him.

00:08:33.260 --> 00:08:36.050
He continued writing bad checks on a daily
basis.

00:08:36.050 --> 00:08:41.800
It didn’t take him long to rationalise that
his time working for $2.75 an hour was not

00:08:41.800 --> 00:08:42.800
worth his effort.

00:08:42.800 --> 00:08:48.920
So, he quit and simply wrote more bad checks.

00:08:48.920 --> 00:08:52.380
Pilot

00:08:52.380 --> 00:08:56.769
One day, while walking along 42nd Street,
Frank was stopped in his tracks at the sight

00:08:56.769 --> 00:09:00.720
of an aircrew from Eastern Airlines emerging
from the Commodore Hotel.

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:06.019
He was mesmerized as the captain, co-pilot,
flight engineer and four stewardesses breezed

00:09:06.019 --> 00:09:07.019
by.

00:09:07.019 --> 00:09:11.480
Frank wrote in his memoir that he had never
seen such a splendid group of people.

00:09:11.480 --> 00:09:12.959
His mind began to work overtime.

00:09:12.959 --> 00:09:14.829
Imagine if he were a pilot.

00:09:14.829 --> 00:09:19.139
He would have instant respect – and, as
a result, would be able to cash checks anywhere

00:09:19.139 --> 00:09:20.949
with impunity.

00:09:20.949 --> 00:09:24.459
Continuing down the street, he found himself
outside of the Pan Am building.

00:09:24.459 --> 00:09:25.940
Quickly he formulated a plan.

00:09:25.940 --> 00:09:30.170
He returned to his accommodation, found Pam
Am’s phone number, rang and asked to speak

00:09:30.170 --> 00:09:32.149
to someone in the purchasing department.

00:09:32.149 --> 00:09:36.710
He went on to inform the purchasing officer
that he was a Pam Am pilot based in Los Angeles.

00:09:36.710 --> 00:09:41.079
He was due to hop a flight back home at 8
pm, but his uniform had been stolen.

00:09:41.079 --> 00:09:43.589
He confidently requested a replacement uniform.

00:09:43.589 --> 00:09:47.500
The helpful purchasing officer gave Frank
directions to the uniform company to pick

00:09:47.500 --> 00:09:48.580
up his uniform.

00:09:48.580 --> 00:09:51.930
He walked out with a complete co-pilot’s
uniform in hand.

00:09:51.930 --> 00:09:54.589
What he needed now was a pilot’s ID card.

00:09:54.589 --> 00:09:58.560
He quickly had one made up, affixing the Pan
Am logo from a model plane that he purchased

00:09:58.560 --> 00:10:01.060
for $2.49.

00:10:01.060 --> 00:10:05.089
Decked out in his Pan Am uniform, Frank walked
into La Guardia airport.

00:10:05.089 --> 00:10:10.270
He was soon approached by a TWA co-pilot who
asked what Pan Am was doing at La Guardia.

00:10:10.270 --> 00:10:13.639
It turned out that Pan Am did not fly out
of La Guardia.

00:10:13.639 --> 00:10:15.500
Picking up on his research, Frank replied
. . .

00:10:15.500 --> 00:10:18.990
‘oh, I just deadheaded in from Frisco on
the first flight I could catch.

00:10:18.990 --> 00:10:20.790
I’ll catch a chopper to Kennedy’

00:10:20.790 --> 00:10:22.259
This answer seemed to satisfy.

00:10:22.259 --> 00:10:25.600
But then the co-pilot asked . . .
What kind of equipment you on?

00:10:25.600 --> 00:10:26.600
This threw Frank.

00:10:26.600 --> 00:10:30.269
In his research, he hadn’t come across the
word equipment used in that context.

00:10:30.269 --> 00:10:32.540
Searching for the right answer, he came out
with . . .

00:10:32.540 --> 00:10:33.730
General Electric.

00:10:33.730 --> 00:10:37.709
The pilot was actually asking what type of
plane Frank flew so the quizzical look on

00:10:37.709 --> 00:10:40.660
his face at this reply was understandable.

00:10:40.660 --> 00:10:42.100
Frank quickly made his departure.

00:10:42.100 --> 00:10:45.000
He didn’t attempt to board a plane that
first day.

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:49.360
He was to spend some weeks getting confident
in his role as he wandered the airport, struck

00:10:49.360 --> 00:10:53.370
up conversations with pilots and built his
store of ‘shop knowledge’.

00:10:53.370 --> 00:10:58.030
During this time, he also found great success
in cashing checks in his pilot’s uniform.

00:10:58.030 --> 00:11:01.360
In fact, the tellers couldn’t give him the
cash fast enough.

00:11:01.360 --> 00:11:04.980
Finally ready to take to the skies, Frank
turned up at La Guardia’s Eastern Operations

00:11:04.980 --> 00:11:09.629
office and informed the desk clerk that he
needed to deadhead to Miami on the next flight,

00:11:09.629 --> 00:11:11.990
assuming that there was room on the plane.

00:11:11.990 --> 00:11:15.769
Deadheading was the name used for the practice
of allowing staff to travel for free in order

00:11:15.769 --> 00:11:17.220
to get to a job location.

00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:20.580
He was told there was a vacancy on a flight
departing in fifteen minutes.

00:11:20.580 --> 00:11:24.939
Within minutes he found himself in the cockpit
sitting in a tiny seat which came down from

00:11:24.939 --> 00:11:25.939
the door behind him.

00:11:25.939 --> 00:11:28.649
With him were the pilot, co-pilot and flight
attendant.

00:11:28.649 --> 00:11:32.120
After asking what type of equipment he flew,
the three pretty much ignored him for the

00:11:32.120 --> 00:11:33.310
rest of the flight.

00:11:33.310 --> 00:11:37.290
Before getting off at Miami, Frank made a
date with one the stewardesses who was laying

00:11:37.290 --> 00:11:39.070
over in Miami that night.

00:11:39.070 --> 00:11:43.600
Amazed at how simple the whole process had
been, Frank became a serial deadheader.

00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:49.100
Upon arriving in a city, he would stay overnight
in a motel used by Pam Am staff, charge the

00:11:49.100 --> 00:11:53.121
bill to the company, and in the morning, before
heading to his next deadhead flight, cash

00:11:53.121 --> 00:11:57.819
a bad check for a hundred or more dollars
just to have some ready cash.

00:11:57.819 --> 00:12:03.670
This pattern continued for two years, between
the ages of sixteen and eighteen, taking him

00:12:03.670 --> 00:12:13.930
to twenty-six countries on more than 250 flights
that covered in excess of a million miles.

00:12:13.930 --> 00:12:17.300
Doctor

00:12:17.300 --> 00:12:20.690
By the middle of 1966, Frank was ready for
a new gig.

00:12:20.690 --> 00:12:24.779
He had experienced a close call when someone
had stated that he didn’t actually work

00:12:24.779 --> 00:12:25.920
for Pan Am.

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:29.999
Fortunately, his bluster and feigned outrage
got him through the challenge but he knew

00:12:29.999 --> 00:12:32.459
that eventually the game would be up.

00:12:32.459 --> 00:12:36.709
It was when applying for a long-term lease
on an apartment in Atlanta, Georgia that he,

00:12:36.709 --> 00:12:40.089
on a whim, wrote down his profession as medical
doctor.

00:12:40.089 --> 00:12:43.259
When asked what type of doctor he was, he
replied that he was a paediatrician, based

00:12:43.259 --> 00:12:48.129
in California but was on a 12-month sabbatical
to audit some research papers.

00:12:48.129 --> 00:12:51.810
Things went well for a period of time, until
one day he got a knock at the door.

00:12:51.810 --> 00:12:57.279
His new neighbor introduced himself – Dr.
Willis Granger, chief resident paediatrician

00:12:57.279 --> 00:13:00.620
at the Smithers General Hospital in Marietta.

00:13:00.620 --> 00:13:04.959
Frank was afraid that Granger would blow his
cover but, fortunately the real doctor wasn’t

00:13:04.959 --> 00:13:09.329
one to talk shop and they were able to cross
each other’s paths without incident.

00:13:09.329 --> 00:13:13.310
Meanwhile Frank spent as much time as he could
at the local library reading up on the subject

00:13:13.310 --> 00:13:14.310
of paediatrics.

00:13:14.310 --> 00:13:19.639
A few weeks later Frank felt confident enough
to accept an offer from Dr. Granger to tour

00:13:19.639 --> 00:13:20.889
Smithers General Hospital.

00:13:20.889 --> 00:13:23.290
After that he made regular visits.

00:13:23.290 --> 00:13:27.510
On one such occasion he was approached by
the hospital administrator and asked to do

00:13:27.510 --> 00:13:32.149
him a huge favor – could he cover a midnight
to eight shift for a doctor who would be away

00:13:32.149 --> 00:13:34.749
for ten days due to a death in the family?

00:13:34.749 --> 00:13:37.939
Frank objected that he didn’t have a licence
to practice in Georgia.

00:13:37.939 --> 00:13:41.839
But the administrator countered that he wouldn’t
really be doing anything during the night

00:13:41.839 --> 00:13:44.720
shift, just overseeing interns and nurses.

00:13:44.720 --> 00:13:49.100
So long as he passed an interview panel of
five doctors everything would be fine.

00:13:49.100 --> 00:13:53.439
Frank’s initial impulse was to refuse – there
were too many dangers involved.

00:13:53.439 --> 00:13:55.750
But he was so intrigued with the challenge.

00:13:55.750 --> 00:13:57.830
And so he agreed.

00:13:57.830 --> 00:14:01.689
Much to Frank’s relief, the interview was
light hearted and non-inquisitorial.

00:14:01.689 --> 00:14:07.089
He was now a night shift paediatric supervising
doctor with 8 interns and 40 nurses under

00:14:07.089 --> 00:14:08.499
his control.

00:14:08.499 --> 00:14:12.870
In order to make it through, Frank took on
the persona of rule-breaking anti-establishment

00:14:12.870 --> 00:14:17.649
practitioner in the mold of Hawkeye Pierce
on his favorite TV show, MASH.

00:14:17.649 --> 00:14:20.190
Things went smoothly until he was called to
the emergency room.

00:14:20.190 --> 00:14:23.660
He took his time getting there, hoping that
an intern would step in.

00:14:23.660 --> 00:14:27.399
When he finally arrived, there were three
interns gathered around the patient.

00:14:27.399 --> 00:14:32.259
Frank asked them for their diagnoses, and
when all three agreed he told then to go ahead

00:14:32.259 --> 00:14:33.259
with the procedure.

00:14:33.259 --> 00:14:37.740
At the end of his ten-day stint, Frank was
approached by the administrator and informed

00:14:37.740 --> 00:14:40.629
that the doctor he was replacing was not coming
back.

00:14:40.629 --> 00:14:43.779
Could he possibly stay on until a replacement
could be found?

00:14:43.779 --> 00:14:51.470
He ended up spending 11 months as a doctor
at Smithers Hospital.

00:14:51.470 --> 00:14:54.850
Lawyer

00:14:54.850 --> 00:14:57.629
After finishing up at the hospital, Frank
decided to leave Atlanta.

00:14:57.629 --> 00:15:01.749
He moved to Baton Rouge, Louisiana where he
met up with a flight steward that he had formerly

00:15:01.749 --> 00:15:03.120
had a relationship with.

00:15:03.120 --> 00:15:07.290
Remembering that Frank had told her about
his (supposed) law degree from Harvard, the

00:15:07.290 --> 00:15:11.720
girl introduced him at a party to assistant
state’s attorney, Jason Wilcox.

00:15:11.720 --> 00:15:15.459
On learning that Frank was currently out of
work, Wilcox told him that the Attorney General

00:15:15.459 --> 00:15:17.879
was looking for bright articulate young lawyers.

00:15:17.879 --> 00:15:21.500
Why didn’t he take the bar exam and practice
with them?

00:15:21.500 --> 00:15:24.809
Once again, Frank found it impossible to resist
the challenge.

00:15:24.809 --> 00:15:27.009
On his third attempt he passed the bar exam.

00:15:27.009 --> 00:15:30.910
He was given a position in one of the Attorney
General’s civil departments.

00:15:30.910 --> 00:15:34.990
The job was really that of a glorified errand
boy, but Frank was happy in the anonymity

00:15:34.990 --> 00:15:36.000
of the role.

00:15:36.000 --> 00:15:39.980
Around this time, he began dating a girl named
Gloria, who was a staunch Methodist.

00:15:39.980 --> 00:15:44.209
Through this relationship he ended up on a
church committee, sitting alongside a man

00:15:44.209 --> 00:15:47.339
who was a real graduate of Harvard Law School.

00:15:47.339 --> 00:15:52.249
This guy began asking too many specific questions
and Frank knew it was time to pull the plug

00:15:52.249 --> 00:15:53.680
on his law career.

00:15:53.680 --> 00:15:57.509
Frank next turned up in Utah, where he claims
to have scammed his way to becoming a teacher

00:15:57.509 --> 00:16:00.170
at Brigham Young University for eight months.

00:16:00.170 --> 00:16:07.519
However, the university denies that he ever
worked there.

00:16:07.519 --> 00:16:09.300
Prisoner

00:16:09.300 --> 00:16:14.350
By now Frank was wanted in many parts of the
world for a long list of fraudulent activities.

00:16:14.350 --> 00:16:17.079
The FBI were on his trail, as was Interpol.

00:16:17.079 --> 00:16:22.089
His luck finally ran out while he was spending
time in the southern French town of Montrichard.

00:16:22.089 --> 00:16:26.459
He was arrested on the basis of an Interpol
warrant for fraud issued from Sweden for fraudulent

00:16:26.459 --> 00:16:30.019
checks written during a visit while he was
supposedly a Pan Am employee.

00:16:30.019 --> 00:16:33.670
The French authorities soon discovered that
he had written false checks all over their

00:16:33.670 --> 00:16:37.120
country also and so they refuse to honor the
extradition.

00:16:37.120 --> 00:16:41.670
Frank was sentenced to 12 months imprisonment
in France, which, after being reduced to 6

00:16:41.670 --> 00:16:44.119
months, was served at Perpignan prison.

00:16:44.119 --> 00:16:49.910
Conditions were abysmal and Frank’s weight
went from 198 to 109 pounds during his stay

00:16:49.910 --> 00:16:50.910
there.

00:16:50.910 --> 00:16:53.740
He was then extradited to Sweden where he
served another six months imprisonment.

00:16:53.740 --> 00:16:58.190
Upon his release from Malmo prison in Sweden,
Frank was taken into custody by US Federal

00:16:58.190 --> 00:17:00.920
officers and returned to the United States.

00:17:00.920 --> 00:17:05.839
After a third trial, he was convicted to twelve
years imprisonment for multiple cases of fraud.

00:17:05.839 --> 00:17:12.200
During his time in US custody he escaped twice
but was recaptured on both occasions.

00:17:12.200 --> 00:17:16.829
Going Straight

00:17:16.829 --> 00:17:20.529
After four years jail, Frank was offered a
deal – his prison term would be quashed

00:17:20.529 --> 00:17:25.909
if he agreed be an on-call consultant to assist
the authorities to investigate fraud cases.

00:17:25.909 --> 00:17:26.909
Frank agreed.

00:17:26.909 --> 00:17:31.649
After his release from prison he took on a
succession of menial jobs, only to be fired

00:17:31.649 --> 00:17:33.870
for not revealing his criminal past.

00:17:33.870 --> 00:17:38.370
He then decided to parlay his vast criminal
experience into a legitimate career by becoming

00:17:38.370 --> 00:17:43.480
a security consultant and advising companies
as to how they could avoid the sorts of scams

00:17:43.480 --> 00:17:45.920
that he had perpetrated for so long.

00:17:45.920 --> 00:17:49.070
He went on to set up his own fraud protection
company, Abagnale and Associates.

00:17:49.070 --> 00:17:55.240
He has been advising the FBI on fraud related
matters for more than 40 years.

00:17:55.240 --> 00:18:00.500
Frank Abagnale, aged 70 in 2018, lives in
Charleston, South Carolina, with his wife

00:18:00.500 --> 00:19:05.920
Kelly.

00:19:05.920 --> 00:19:49.880
They have three sons, one of whom works for

00:19:49.880 --> 00:19:50.220
the FBI.

